From b785a78a5927f5ab3930063982d0de34df7831c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:57:43 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Add the stereoscopic 3D packing to QVideoFrameFormat and fill
 it in the FFmpeg backend

A stereoscopic 3D video holds both views in every frame, in one of the
packings the frame packing arrangement SEI of H.264 (Table D-8) and H.265
(Table D.8) names (checkerboard 0, column interleaving 1, row interleaving 2,
side by side 3, top and bottom 4, temporal interleaving 5, two-dimensional 6,
and the quincunx sampling flag of side by side), or Matroska's StereoMode
(RFC 9559, 5.1.4.1.28.3, values 0 to 14: the same packings with the eye order,
and the red/cyan and green/magenta anaglyphs). FFmpeg reads the SEI and the
StereoMode into the Stereo 3D side data of the decoded frames, as an AVStereo3D
(type, the invert flag for the right eye first, the view for a frame of a
sequence); only the anaglyphs, which have no AVStereo3D type, stay in the
stream's stereo_mode tag. Nothing in Qt Multimedia passed any of it on, so a
player built on it could not tell a 3D video from a wide or a tall one, or
turn its views into what its display needs.

Add StereoMode (None, SideBySide, TopBottom, SideBySideQuincunx, RowInterleaved,
ColumnInterleaved, Checkerboard, FrameSequence, AnaglyphCyanRed,
AnaglyphGreenMagenta), StereoFlag::RightEyeFirst and StereoView (which view a
frame of a frame sequence holds) to QVideoFrameFormat. The FFmpeg backend sets
them from the frame's side data, as it already does for the mastering display's
luminance, and from the stream's tag for the anaglyphs. The SEI is sent with
keyframes only and holds until another replaces it (H.264 D.2.26,
frame_packing_arrangement_repetition_period 1), so the video renderer keeps the
last packing it saw.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
